WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:03.130
<v ->Okay, so now let's talk about the security</v>

2
00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:04.640
of our VPC endpoints.

3
00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.090
And they're called VPC Endpoint Policies.

4
00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.320
So VPC Endpoint Policy, as you can guess,

5
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.320
is going to be a JSON document

6
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.840
to control access to a service.

7
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.483
And this looks just like this.

8
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.400
So in this example, what do we have?

9
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:23.060
Well, we have a policy that allows us to do SQS SendMessage

10
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:26.750
to a specific queue using a specific principle,

11
00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.300
which represents a user through IM.

12
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.070
So this is applied to a VPC endpoint.

13
00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:36.440
That means that only that user can access this queue

14
00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:37.960
through that VPC endpoint,

15
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.850
and it has to be an SQS SendMessage.

16
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.640
So this does not override or replace IM user policies

17
00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.870
or service specific policies,

18
00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.131
so you may still have an SQS queue policy

19
00:00:48.131 --> 00:00:52.510
and the IM user still needs to be allowed to talk to SQS.

20
00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.960
But this is yet another level of protection.

21
00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.530
This one is happening at the VPC endpoint level.

22
00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:03.490
So for notes, is that if our IM user is trying to access SQS

23
00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.840
out of our VPC endpoint using the public route,

24
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.110
then it may still work,

25
00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:11.020
because the VPC endpoint policy will not apply.

26
00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.290
In this case what we could do,

27
00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:16.230
is add an SQS queue policy to deny any action

28
00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.820
that is not done through the VPC endpoint,

29
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.690
effectively forcing any connection

30
00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:22.300
to go through the VPC endpoint.

31
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.970
And then forcing the VPC endpoint policy

32
00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:27.090
to be applied no matter what.

33
00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.440
Okay, so another way you can apply

34
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.810
a VPC endpoint policy is going to be on an S3 bucket.

35
00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.150
So here is an example.

36
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:37.240
In this example, we are restricting access,

37
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.600
through the VPC endpoint only,

38
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.573
to one specific bucket called my_secure_bucket.

39
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:44.780
So here we're saying,

40
00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.940
anyone can do GetObject and PutObject

41
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.050
on and only on my_secure_bucket.

42
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:52.080
Hopefully that makes sense.

43
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:52.940
But if you can't remember,

44
00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.260
this VPC endpoint policy is applied

45
00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:56.980
at the VPC endpoint level.

46
00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:59.500
And one can circumvent it, if they want to go

47
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.200
through Amazon S3 through the public route.

48
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.050
So here is another VPC endpoint policy.

49
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.290
And this time it's to only allow access

50
00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.430
to Amazon Linux 2 repositories.

51
00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:10.690
So the idea is,

52
00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:13.640
when our EC2 instances need to do an update

53
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.620
of their Linux operating system,

54
00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.060
what actually goes on is that they are getting some objects

55
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.590
on an S3 bucket that belongs to Amazon.

56
00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:24.550
And so, this VPC endpoint policy allows your EC2 instances

57
00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:27.590
to have access privately to this repository

58
00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.380
through your VPC endpoint policy.

59
00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:33.440
Okay, so we can combine your VPC endpoint policy

60
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.320
and your S3 bucket policy.

61
00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:39.090
So your S3 bucket policy may have some specific conditions.

62
00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:44.090
And this one example is aws:sourceVpce equals this VPC ID

63
00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.160
to deny any traffic that doesn't come

64
00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.130
from a specific VPC endpoint.

65
00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:48.963
So here we're saying,

66
00:02:48.963 --> 00:02:52.060
if we have this condition in our S3 bucket policy,

67
00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:53.530
then any traffic going through

68
00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.760
our VPC endpoint is going to be authorized.

69
00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.980
And any traffic not going through the VPC endpoint,

70
00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:59.540
is going to be denied.

71
00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:02.580
So effectively, we've locked down our S3 bucket

72
00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:05.930
to be only usable from our VPC endpoints.

73
00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.010
And if we have many VPC endpoints,

74
00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:12.270
and we want to be able to have a more global condition,

75
00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:16.830
then we can use the condition aws:sourceVpc equals this,

76
00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.670
for a specific VPC.

77
00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:20.010
But here's the catch,

78
00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:23.330
the aws:sourceVpc condition only works

79
00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:26.230
for VPC endpoints and for private traffic.

80
00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:28.300
So this is when you have multiple endpoints

81
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.420
and you want to manage access to your S3 bucket

82
00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.080
for all your endpoints.

83
00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.470
So it only works when you go through VPC endpoints.

84
00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:39.240
So that means that if you want to restrict access using IP,

85
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.490
the only way to do it,

86
00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:44.740
is to use a specific public IP address or an elastic IP.

87
00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:47.950
You cannot use this SourceIp condition

88
00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.570
to restrict based on a private IP.

89
00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:55.070
So remember, if you are having a private VPC endpoint

90
00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:58.320
and you want to restrict access using private IP,

91
00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:03.320
you have to use aws:SourceVpce or SourceVpc as a condition.

92
00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.310
But if you want to restrict based on the public IP,

93
00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:09.680
then you have to use the aws:SourceIP.

94
00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.880
The aws:SourceIP only applies to public IPs,

95
00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:15.830
and the other ones only apply to private network traffic.

96
00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.870
So let's have a look through an example

97
00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:19.580
for S3 bucket policy.

98
00:04:19.580 --> 00:04:22.690
So this S3 bucket policy restricts your access

99
00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:26.590
to your S3 bucket through only one specific VPC endpoint.

100
00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:29.780
So we're saying principal, star, action, star, effect,

101
00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:34.350
deny anything that doesn't come from the source VPCE

102
00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.170
that we have specified here.

103
00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:39.590
Or, here is an example where we're denying any traffic

104
00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:41.870
that doesn't come from the source VPC

105
00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:43.140
and the source VPC we have.

106
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.430
This is in case we have multiple VPC endpoints.

107
00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:49.590
So finally, how can we troubleshoot a connection

108
00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:54.590
from an instance in a private subnet in a VPC to Amazon S3?

109
00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:56.560
And, as you can see,

110
00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.760
there's going to be a lot of moving parts.

111
00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.230
But just before I do so,

112
00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:02.280
if you want to do it as an exercise yourself,

113
00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.380
try to see all the possible things that can go wrong

114
00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:07.140
when trying to connect an EC2 instance

115
00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:11.400
in a private subnet onto an Amazon S3 bucket privately.

116
00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.970
Okay, so let's get started.

117
00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:14.660
First one, is that we have to check

118
00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.060
the security group of our instance.

119
00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.190
The security group has outbound rules.

120
00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:22.630
And if the outbound rules are not allowing the traffic out,

121
00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:24.460
then things will not work.

122
00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:27.410
Then we have to create our VPC endpoint gateway,

123
00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:29.210
because we are accessing Amazon S3.

124
00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:31.560
And so, our VPC endpoint gateway will have

125
00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.370
a VPC endpoint policy.

126
00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.290
And it needs to allow, obviously,

127
00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:37.853
our EC2 instance to access Amazon S3.

128
00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:41.890
Then we need to make sure we update the route tables

129
00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:44.490
to make sure that anything that is requested

130
00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:46.610
for Amazon S3 is going to go

131
00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:48.740
through the VPC endpoint gateway.

132
00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.410
If we don't set up the route table,

133
00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:52.000
then our EC2 instance can never talk

134
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.950
to the VPC endpoint gateway, and things will not work.

135
00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:57.800
And also, we need to make sure that the VPC DNS settings

136
00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.050
for the DNS resolution are enabled

137
00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.720
for the resolution to be correctly done.

138
00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.980
Next, once we go through the VPC endpoint gateway,

139
00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:08.220
we are getting into the Amazon S3 bucket.

140
00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:09.670
And here we have to verify

141
00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:12.680
that the Amazon S3 bucket policy is indeed allowing

142
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:15.740
our EC2 instance to access Amazon S3.

143
00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:16.780
And then finally,

144
00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:19.410
our EC2 instance may have an EC2 instance role.

145
00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.261
And that role may allow it, or may not allow it,

146
00:06:22.261 --> 00:06:23.470
to access the S3 bucket.

147
00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:25.850
So we also have to check IAM permissions.

148
00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:26.683
And that's it.

149
00:06:26.683 --> 00:06:28.690
As you can see, there's a lot of moving parts

150
00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:30.760
that can restrict your EC2 instance

151
00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:32.810
from connecting to your Amazon S3 bucket.

152
00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:35.750
And understanding that, is key going into the exam.

153
00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:38.550
So that's it for me, I will see you in the next lecture.

